+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: nalgebra
+
+Property-based tests written with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 (library crate name:
+`hegel`), added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest -p nalgebra` (workspace
+member; nalgebra's `rust-version = 1.89` is above hegeltest's MSRV, no
+override needed).
+
+nalgebra already has a substantial proptest suite (`tests/proptest/mod.rs`
+strategies plus `proptest!` blocks in most `tests/linalg/*.rs` files), all
+gated behind the `proptest-support` feature and run in CI with
+`--features ...,proptest-support,...`. The hegel tests **supplement** it
+rather than port it: the proptest suite checks the same decompositions but
+with blanket `1.0e-5`/`1.0e-7` epsilons and `new_random()` inputs
+(uniform [0,1) entries — never negative, tiny, zero-heavy, or
+boundary-shaped). The hegel tests draw dimensions 1..=6 and entries from
+[-100, 100] **including ±0, subnormals, and tiny magnitudes**, and set every
+tolerance from an explicit conditioning argument. That difference in input
+distribution is exactly where all three bugs below were found.
+
+Tests live in `mod hegel_props` at the end of the existing test files
+(run with `cargo test --features proptest-support,arbitrary`, or the full
+CI feature set; `tests/lib.rs` requires `debug,compare,rand,macros`, which
+the self-dev-dependency enables, and `qr.rs`/`cholesky.rs`/`solve.rs` are
+file-gated on `proptest-support`):
+
+- `tests/lib.rs` — shared helpers in `mod utils`: `draw_dim` (1..=6),
+  `draw_dmatrix` (entries in [-100, 100]), `fro_norm_upper`
+  (overflow/underflow-safe Frobenius upper bound for tolerance formulas),
+  `zero_tiny_entries` (pinned-bug exclusion, documented there)
+- `tests/linalg/qr.rs` (2 properties + 1 pinned bug)
+- `tests/linalg/lu.rs` (4 properties + 1 pinned bug)
+- `tests/linalg/cholesky.rs` (1 property)
+- `tests/linalg/svd.rs` (2 properties + 1 pinned bug)
+- `tests/linalg/inverse.rs` (2 properties)
+- `tests/linalg/eigen.rs` (1 property)
+- `tests/core/matrix.rs` (2 properties)
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All are oracle-independent (reconstruction / structural / residual — the
+test holds A and needs no external reference), except none: no differentials
+were needed.
+
+| # | Test | Property | Tolerance argument |
+|---|------|----------|--------------------|
+| 1 | `qr.rs qr_reconstructs_matrix` | `Q * R ≈ A` (rectangular, dims drawn independently) | Householder QR backward stability: `32 * n * eps * ‖A‖_F`, floored at `f64::MIN_POSITIVE` |
+| 2 | `qr.rs qr_q_is_orthonormal` | `Qᵀ Q ≈ I` | `32 * n * eps * sqrt(min(r,c))` — Q entries are O(1), no ‖A‖ dependence |
+| 3 | `lu.rs lu_reconstructs_permuted_matrix` | `P⁻¹ (L U) ≈ A` | LU backward stability with partial-pivoting growth: `32 * n * 2^n * eps * ‖A‖` |
+| 4 | `lu.rs lu_solve_residual_is_small` | `A x ≈ b` for `x = lu.solve(b)` | Backward-stable solve: residual `<= c * n * g * eps * (‖A‖‖x‖ + ‖b‖)` — self-scales with ‖x‖, so **no condition-number skip needed**; skips only exact singularity (`solve` = None) and true solution overflow (`x` non-finite) |
+| 5 | `lu.rs determinant_product_rule` | `det(A B) ≈ det(A) det(B)` | Relative error of an LU determinant is `~ c * n * g * eps * kappa(M)` (first-order perturbation of det through the backward error); kappa₂ measured from singular values, `kappa(AB) <= kappa(A) kappa(B)`; skips when the resulting rel-tol exceeds 1e-3 (identity not checkable in f64) |
+| 6 | `lu.rs determinant_of_transpose_matches` | `det(Aᵀ) ≈ det(A)` (different pivot sequences / cofactor groupings) | Same kappa-scaled relative argument as #5 |
+| 7 | `cholesky.rs cholesky_reconstructs_spd_matrix` | For `A = MᵀM + n·I` (SPD by construction, `lambda_min >= n`): `L Lᵀ ≈ A`, and `cholesky()` **must** succeed (the `expect` is part of the property) | Cholesky on SPD is backward stable with no growth factor: `32 * n * eps * ‖A‖` |
+| 8 | `svd.rs svd_reconstructs_matrix` | `U Σ Vᵀ ≈ A` | Intended: `128 * n * eps * ‖A‖` (iterative, generous constant). Currently `8 * n * sqrt(eps) * ‖A‖` — the defect ceiling of pinned bug #3 below, since the buggy 2x2 block arises mid-iteration and cannot be excluded at the input |
+| 9 | `svd.rs svd_singular_values_nonnegative_and_descending` | All singular values `>= 0` and sorted descending | Exact structural contract (implementation sorts explicitly; crate's own `svd_sorted` test relies on it) |
+| 10 | `inverse.rs inverse_composes_to_identity` | `A A⁻¹ ≈ I` when `try_inverse` succeeds (dynamic, LU path) | Per-column backward-stable solve: `64 * n * 2^n * eps * ‖A‖‖A⁻¹‖` — kappa is *in the formula*; additionally skips kappa_F > 1e12 (condition threshold, per task guidance) |
+| 11 | `inverse.rs inverse_composes_to_identity_static4` | Same for static `Matrix4` (dedicated cofactor formula — different code path) | Adjugate error analysis: `64 * eps * (‖A‖⁴/|det| + ‖A‖‖A⁻¹‖)`; skips `‖A‖⁴/|det| > 1e14` (cofactor formula's own condition threshold) |
+| 12 | `eigen.rs symmetric_eigen_pairs_satisfy_eigen_equation` | For `A = (M + Mᵀ)/2` (exactly symmetric): `A vᵢ ≈ lambdaᵢ vᵢ` for every computed eigenpair | Symmetric QR residual bound `128 * n * eps * ‖A‖` for unit-norm v — eigen *residuals* are governed by backward stability alone (no kappa, unlike eigenvalue accuracy for clustered spectra) |
+| 13 | `matrix.rs transpose_involution_dyn` | `(Aᵀ)ᵀ == A` exactly (drawn dims; existing proptest only covers fixed 2x3/3x3) | Exact — data movement only |
+| 14 | `matrix.rs product_transpose_is_reversed_transpose_product` | `(A B)ᵀ ≈ Bᵀ Aᵀ` | Same dot products, possibly different accumulation order: reassociation error `<= 64 * k * eps * ‖A‖_F ‖B‖_F` |
+
+Norm factors in all tolerance formulas use `fro_norm_upper`
+(`max|a_ij| * sqrt(r*c)`) rather than `Matrix::norm()`, because `norm()`
+underflows to 0 for subnormal-scale entries (squares without scaling) and
+would collapse a norm-scaled tolerance to its floor — that protects the
+tests' arithmetic, not any nalgebra contract.
+
+## Test results
+
+- **14/14 properties pass** at the default case count and at a 10x
+  exploratory run (`test_cases = 1000`, temporarily applied and reverted;
+  the run was verified live with a per-case counter — 1000 cases — rather
+  than wall-clock).
+- **3 pinned known-failure tests fail deterministically** (concrete inputs,
+  plain `#[test]`s), documenting the real bugs below.
+- Guarded branches verified non-vacuous at the 10x run with temporary
+  fire counters: LU solve guard fired 932/1000, det-product 850/1000,
+  det-transpose 899/1000, dynamic inverse 891/1000, static inverse
+  968/1000. The non-finite-x overflow guard fired 0/1000 (pure safety
+  guard; its trigger was observed once during bring-up).
+- Liveness check: assertions in `qr_q_is_orthonormal` and
+  `inverse_composes_to_identity` (inside the guard) were temporarily
+  inverted; both failed with minimal shrunk counterexamples (1x1 zero /
+  identity matrices), then reverted.
+- Full suite (`cargo test -p nalgebra --features proptest-support,arbitrary`):
+  530 passed, 4 failed = the 3 pins + 1 **pre-existing failure**
+  (see below).
+
+**Pre-existing failure (unrelated to this work):**
+`macros::stack::stack_trybuild_tests` fails on the pristine checkout
+(verified via `git stash`): 2 of 4 trybuild cases report diagnostics that
+differ from the checked-in `.stderr` expectations under rustc 1.95.0
+(trybuild suggests `TRYBUILD=overwrite` to re-bless). This is
+toolchain-version brittleness in the macro error-message snapshots, not a
+behavioral regression.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All three are oracle-independent reconstruction failures on in-domain
+finite inputs, found by the properties above, shrunk by hegel, and
+independently reproduced in a scratch crate against the library source.
+
+### 1. QR: Q/R sign inconsistency for tiny negative pivot columns (residual 2.0)
+
+Pinned: `tests/linalg/qr.rs qr_reconstruction_known_failure_tiny_negative_pivot`.
+
+`A = [-1.2477274453361457e-162, 1.0]` (1x2) gives `Q = [-1]`,
+`R = [0, 1]`, so `Q*R = [-0, -1]` — `‖QR - A‖ = 2.0` instead of O(eps).
+
+Root cause (`src/linalg/householder.rs::reflection_axis_mut` +
+`src/linalg/qr.rs::q`): the pivot column's `norm_squared()` (1.56e-324)
+underflows to 0, so `factor == 0` and "no reflection necessary" is
+reported — R's trailing columns are left untouched — but the returned
+signed norm is `sign(-) * 0.0 = -0.0`, and `q()` later applies
+`reflect_with_sign(..., diag.signum())` where `signum(-0.0) = -1`,
+flipping a Q column that the factorization never applied to R. Positive
+tiny entries and exact ±0.0 are handled consistently; any pivot column
+with a tiny *negative* leading entry and underflowing norm-squared
+triggers it.
+
+### 2. LU: NaN factors for finite matrices with subnormal pivots
+
+Pinned: `tests/linalg/lu.rs lu_reconstruction_known_failure_subnormal_pivot`.
+
+`A = [[0, 0], [5.562684646268003e-309, 0]]` (finite) yields
+`L = [[1, 0], [NaN, 1]]`, `U = [[5.56e-309, 0], [0, NaN]]` — the
+reconstruction is NaN.
+
+Root cause (`src/linalg/lu.rs::gauss_step`/`gauss_step_swap`):
+`let inv_diag = T::one() / diag; coeffs *= inv_diag;`. When
+`|pivot| < 1/f64::MAX` (~5.6e-309) the reciprocal overflows to `inf` and
+the zero entries below the pivot become `0 * inf = NaN`, which propagates.
+LAPACK's `dgetrf` guards exactly this case with an `abs(pivot) >= sfmin`
+test, falling back to true division. Also poisons `solve`, `determinant`
+and `try_inverse` on such inputs (all LU-based).
+
+### 3. SVD: catastrophic cancellation in the trailing-2x2 solver (residual ~sqrt(eps)·‖A‖)
+
+Pinned: `tests/linalg/svd.rs svd_reconstruction_known_failure_2x2_cancellation`.
+
+Dynamic 2x2 `A = [[2.2739560367948712, -2.5014725597524403e-6], [0, 9.00831238235618]]`
+reconstructs with residual 1.23e-8 instead of ~1e-14.
+
+Root cause (`src/linalg/svd.rs::compute_2x2_uptrig_svd`): the V rotation is
+computed from `v1*v1 - m11*m11`. The code comment says v1 (the singular
+value nearest m22) was chosen precisely so this difference does not
+cancel — but `v1 = 2*m11*m22/denom` is sigma_min, which is nearest **m11**
+whenever `m11 < m22`. In that ordering the difference cancels
+catastrophically and the rotation loses half its digits. Sweeping the
+off-diagonal shows the residual peaks at ~`sqrt(eps) * ‖A‖` (off-diag
+~1e-6..1e-8) and equals |off-diag| below that. The statically-sized
+`Matrix2::svd` does not use this routine and reconstructs to 2e-16, which
+is also evidence the dynamic path's behavior is unintended. U/V remain
+orthogonal and the singular values themselves are accurate — only the
+rotation angles (hence reconstruction) are wrong. Because the 2x2 block
+arises mid-iteration for any input size, the general property #8 runs at
+the defect ceiling (`8 * n * sqrt(eps) * ‖A‖`) with a comment to restore
+the eps-level tolerance after the fix.
+
+Not reported as a bug: `lu.solve` returning `Some([inf])` when the true
+solution's magnitude exceeds `f64::MAX` (e.g. 1x1 `[1e-308] x = [13.6]`) —
+faithful overflow, guarded in test #4 with a comment; and `Matrix::norm()`
+underflowing to 0 for subnormal-scale matrices (unscaled sum of squares) —
+arguably a quality-of-implementation issue worth mentioning upstream, but
+no documented contract promises scaled norms, so the tests route around it
+via `fro_norm_upper` instead of asserting on it.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Strict backward-stability tolerance for the SVD reconstruction**
+  (`128 * n * eps * ‖A‖`): sound in theory, but currently fails on real
+  bug #3; kept in a comment on property #8 and asserted in the pinned test
+  so the tight form returns when the 2x2 solver is fixed. Not abandoned as
+  unsound so much as parked behind the known defect.
+- **`det(Lᵀ) == det(L)` exactly** (from the task sketch): unsound —
+  nalgebra's `determinant()` runs LU (or, for n <= 3, cofactor formulas
+  whose transposed evaluation groups products differently), so even
+  triangular inputs give only kappa-scaled approximate equality. Replaced
+  by property #6 with the kappa-scaled tolerance.
+- **Condition-number skip for the LU solve residual** (task sketch
+  suggested a determinant/condition threshold): unnecessary and weaker
+  than what backward stability provides — the residual bound scaled by
+  `‖A‖‖x̂‖` holds for arbitrarily ill-conditioned invertible systems, so
+  the test skips only exact singularity and solution overflow. Documented
+  in the test comment.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The float-tolerance-at-extremes guidance ("the test's own oracle
+  overflows first") was load-bearing three separate times here, but the
+  failing mode was **underflow of the library-provided norm**, not the
+  test's own arithmetic overflow: `Matrix::norm()` returning exactly 0 for
+  a nonzero subnormal matrix silently collapsed `eps * ‖A‖` tolerances to
+  the `MIN_POSITIVE` floor and produced false failures at the 10x run
+  only. A reference bullet like "if the tolerance's scale factor comes
+  from the *library under test* (a `.norm()`/`.abs().max()` call), check
+  it is over/underflow-safe before trusting a boundary failure" would have
+  saved one investigation round-trip.
+- The "oracle-independent failures are almost always real" triage weight
+  held perfectly: all three failures were reconstruction identities on
+  inputs the test still held, and all three turned out to be genuine
+  (sign inconsistency, NaN poisoning, cancellation) in a mature,
+  heavily-tested crate whose own proptest suite misses them because its
+  `new_random()` inputs are uniform [0,1).
+- The 10x exploratory run earned its keep: two of the three bugs (LU NaN)
+  and both test-arithmetic underflow issues only appeared at
+  `test_cases = 1000`, not at the default 100.
+- Per-test `zero_tiny_entries`-style exclusion helpers with the pinned
+  test cross-referenced in a doc comment (rather than inline magic maps)
+  kept the sibling-exclusion rule auditable; worth showing as a pattern in
+  the reference.
